WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.119
Welcome back. When you build a multi-agent system, the way you wire the agents together

2
00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:10.479
shapes almost everything downstream. How work is coordinated, how failures are handled,

3
00:00:10.479 --> 00:00:16.399
and whether you can audit what happened. Coming up in this video, we'll cover three core patterns,

4
00:00:16.399 --> 00:00:22.479
where each wins and breaks, how to pick between them, and the evaluator optimizer loop,

5
00:00:22.479 --> 00:00:26.079
and two anti-patterns that you need to watch out for. Let's dig in.

6
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.639
Pick the wrong wiring and you will feel it for the life of the system,

7
00:00:30.639 --> 00:00:36.560
because topology is notoriously hard to change once agents depend on it. There are three shapes

8
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.319
worth knowing. Hub and spoke puts a central orchestrator in charge, routing every task

9
00:00:42.319 --> 00:00:48.080
and every message out to peripheral agents. The spokes never talk to each other. A pipeline

10
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.840
arranges agents in a line, where each one takes the previous agent's output and hands its own

11
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.360
result forward, a chain of specialized transformations. Peer-to-peer is the opposite

12
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:05.760
of hub and spoke. Agents talk directly, with no coordinator in the middle and no single node

13
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.360
controlling communication. Different structures, different strengths, and very different ways of

14
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.919
failing. Hub and spoke puts one orchestrator in charge of coordination. Work goes from the hub

15
00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:23.519
out to a specialist, results come back to the hub, and spokes never communicate with each other.

16
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.360
That structure buys you centralized control, which means a single place to enforce policy,

17
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:35.040
track state, and audit every decision the system made. When something goes wrong, you know where

18
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.680
to look. Failure handling stays simple, because the hub owns recovery for all of its spokes.

19
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.000
If your workflow needs a clear delegation structure and a complete audit trail,

20
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.199
this topology gives you both by construction rather than by discipline, and that is a

21
00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:57.519
meaningful difference when a system grows. Everything that makes hub and spoke attractive

22
00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:03.599
is also where it breaks. The hub is a single point of failure. If it goes down or becomes

23
00:02:03.599 --> 00:02:09.440
unreachable, the whole system halts, because nothing else can coordinate the work. As you

24
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.800
add spokes, the hub turns into a throughput bottleneck. Every message passes through it,

25
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.720
so its capacity sets the ceiling for the entire system, and the hub takes on context bloat over

26
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:27.600
time, since it has to track state for every spoke currently working. None of these are bugs.

27
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.880
They are the direct cost of centralizing control, and you are trading resilience away

28
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.839
to get simplicity. That trade is often correct. It should just be deliberate.

29
00:02:39.039 --> 00:02:44.960
A pipeline arranges agents in a strict sequence. The first processes input and passes output to

30
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:51.119
the second, the second passes to the third, and so on down the chain. Every stage carries

31
00:02:51.119 --> 00:02:57.440
a narrow responsibility, which makes stages modular. You can swap, test, or replace one

32
00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.960
without touching the rest. Each stage emits a clean artifact that becomes the next stage's

33
00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:10.240
full input. The costs come from the same linearity. Total latency is the sum of every stage,

34
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.679
because nothing overlaps. And a failed middle stage blocks everything downstream,

35
00:03:15.679 --> 00:03:21.360
since those stages have no input to work on. The deeper the chain, the more surface a single

36
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:28.639
failure has. Handle errors at every boundary, not just at the end. Peer-to-peer removes the

37
00:03:28.639 --> 00:03:34.000
coordinator completely. Agents talk directly to each other, and that buys two real advantages.

38
00:03:34.559 --> 00:03:40.160
Latency drops, because messages no longer detour through a central node. And the system gets more

39
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:46.000
resilient, because there is no single point whose failure stops everything. One agent going down

40
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.559
leaves the others working. The costs are equally real. With no central log and no central state,

41
00:03:52.559 --> 00:03:58.639
auditing gets hard. Tracing a single decision means inspecting every agent that participated

42
00:03:58.639 --> 00:04:04.960
rather than reading one coordinator's history. Emergent behavior becomes a genuine concern too,

43
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:11.039
since complex interactions between agents are harder to predict, test, and control when nothing

44
00:04:11.039 --> 00:04:17.440
is managing overall execution. Putting peer-to-peer and hub-and-spoke side by side

45
00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.720
makes the trade-off concrete, because they sit at opposite ends of the same spectrum.

46
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.720
Peer-to-peer has no single point of failure, so any agent can drop without halting the system.

47
00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.320
And it has no routing bottleneck, so throughput scales with the number of agents rather than with

48
00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.239
one coordinator's capacity. What it gives up is central state. Coordination is distributed,

49
00:04:40.239 --> 00:04:45.440
which makes the system harder to reason about and much harder to audit. Hub-and-spoke makes

50
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:51.600
the opposite bet. One node owns state, recovery, and the decision log, and you pay for that with

51
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.839
a throughput ceiling and a component that takes everything down with it. Here is the part that

52
00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:04.079
turns all of this into a decision. Before picking a topology, map the task's data flow because the

53
00:05:04.079 --> 00:05:10.480
structure usually chooses for you. Ask whether there is a natural hierarchy, one agent whose job

54
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:17.839
is to decompose goals and delegate to specialists. If so, that is hub-and-spoke. Ask whether ordering

55
00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:24.399
is forced by the data itself, each step genuinely needing the previous step's output. If so, that is

56
00:05:24.399 --> 00:05:30.239
a pipeline. Ask whether the agents are loosely coupled, with no shared state and no required

57
00:05:30.239 --> 00:05:36.720
ordering. If so, peer-to-peer fits. Then check auditability, because it overrides all three.

58
00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:42.079
If you need a complete decision log, peer-to-peer is off the table, regardless of how well it fits

59
00:05:42.079 --> 00:05:50.239
otherwise. The evaluator optimizer is a two-agent loop worth knowing as its own pattern. A generator

60
00:05:50.239 --> 00:05:56.880
produces an initial result, which might be a draft, a plan, or an answer. An evaluator critiques that

61
00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:03.119
result against a rubric and hands-back structured feedback. The generator revises, and the loop runs

62
00:06:03.119 --> 00:06:09.839
again. This continues until the output clears a quality threshold or hits a maximum iteration

63
00:06:09.839 --> 00:06:16.239
count. That iteration cap isn't optional. Without it, the loop has no guaranteed stopping point,

64
00:06:16.239 --> 00:06:22.559
and can run indefinitely, burning tokens on diminishing refinements. Explicit convergence

65
00:06:22.559 --> 00:06:28.559
criteria, meaning a concrete definition of good enough, and an explicit termination condition

66
00:06:28.559 --> 00:06:35.920
are both required parts of any real implementation. Two anti-patterns come up often enough to name.

67
00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:42.399
The first is a hub without hierarchy, meaning hub and spoke forced onto a task that has none.

68
00:06:42.959 --> 00:06:48.720
Every message routes through a coordinator with no actual coordination to do, so you have bought

69
00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.880
a bottleneck and added latency without gaining any control. The agents could have talked directly.

70
00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.720
The second is P2P without an audit trail, reaching for peer-to-peer when a complete decision log is

71
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:07.519
required. Distributed state makes that log impossible to reconstruct afterward, and instrumentation

72
00:07:07.519 --> 00:07:14.079
only partly compensates. If compliance, debugging, or accountability need it, peer-to-peer can't meet

73
00:07:14.079 --> 00:07:19.839
the requirement by design. Neither announces itself early. They surface once the system is

74
00:07:19.839 --> 00:07:26.959
already in production. Real systems combine these patterns because a complex task rarely has one

75
00:07:26.959 --> 00:07:33.760
uniform shape. A hub and spoke orchestrator can delegate into an internal pipeline for the parts

76
00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:40.239
that genuinely need ordered transforms. A pipeline stage can spawn peer-to-peer agents for a batch of

77
00:07:40.239 --> 00:07:46.959
loosely coupled subtasks. Hybrids are legitimate designs, not compromises. What they add is

78
00:07:46.959 --> 00:07:53.760
coordination complexity at every seam between patterns, and each seam needs an explicit handoff.

79
00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:59.519
What does the output look like leaving one pattern? What happens when the transition fails,

80
00:07:59.519 --> 00:08:05.839
and how does state move across? The discipline that keeps hybrids workable is naming which

81
00:08:05.839 --> 00:08:12.160
subsystem each pattern serves before you design the boundaries between them. Routing everything

82
00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:18.720
through a coordinator isn't the same as coordinating. When the task has no real hierarchy, that hub is

83
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:25.679
just a bottleneck wearing a manager's badge. The data flow already tells you the shape. Ask whether

84
00:08:25.679 --> 00:08:31.519
ordering is forced, whether the agents are loosely coupled, and whether you'll need a decision log

85
00:08:31.519 --> 00:08:38.239
later. Read that honestly and the wiring will pick itself for you. That closes out this lesson.

86
00:08:38.239 --> 00:08:39.599
I'll see you in the next one.

